  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nOur approach leverages semi-definite programming (SDP) to transform the non-convex optimization problem into a convex relaxation. By maximizing the minimum cross-correlation across shifts, we aim to minimize the maximum overlap, which corresponds to a lower bound for C\u2085. This method provides a structured and mathematically grounded approach, ensuring the solution satisfies all constraints and is globally optimal within the relaxed framework.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe define variables h[i] and introduce a new matrix variable Y, where Y[i,j] = h[i]h[j]. The problem constraints become:\n- Y[i,j] = h[i]h[j] for all i,j\n- Y is positive semi-definite (PSD)\n- sum(Y[i,i+1]) \u2014 a placeholder for sliding sums \u2014 \u2265 t * dx for each shift k\n- sum(h) * dx = 1\nThe objective is to maximize t, minimizing C\u2085 = 1 - t.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nThe steps involve:\na. Initializing n_points from the provided h or defaulting to 70.\nb. Setting up the SDP with CVXPY to maximize t, ensuring Y is PSD and constraints are met.\nc. Solving the SDP and checking if Y can be decomposed into h.\nd. Adjusting h to meet the sum constraint and projecting to [0,1].\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nWe explore variations in solver options, convexity enforcement techniques, and ways to decompose Y into h, ensuring robustness across different starting points.\n\n5. Compute budget\nThe implementation within 1100s involves high-dimensional operations, with an estimated 750 iterations of SDP solving. Dynamic adjustment of solver precision and warm-starts from initial_h_values enhance efficiency.\n\n6. Validation\nPost-processing checks confirm h's feasibility, computes the exact C\u2085, and handles fallbacks if the SDP solution doesn't yield a valid h.",
